"Kent Paul Dolan" <xanthian@well.com> writes:
> Trying to make sense of code where whole functions fail to have a
> descriptive header is "interesting", but in mhimm.c, it _looks_ like
> it is possible for a nymph to teleport to you, attack (probably your
> pet, under the circumstances), fail, teleport away completely out of
> sight, all in one move

Not as far as I can see. Nymphs will only teleport themselves on a
successful theft from another monster (the AD_SITM/AD_SEDU case of
mdamagem(), guarded by an if(obj)), a forestalled theft from you
(you're a nymph or foocubus yourself or (2/3 chance) the nymph is
cancelled), a successful theft from you or your having nothing to
steal (steal() returns 1 or schedules a stealarm() call), or as their
move (the usual 1/40 chance for a teleporting monster; if they do
this, they don't do anything else such as attacking).

> thus _never_ be visible, since the screen doesn't update
> synchonously, and the "you hear noises" message have the "farq"
> attribute that make the message include "in the distance", because
> her position isn't checked until the time for the sound message to
> be produced occurs.

noises() is called _before_ mdamagem(), so won't be affected by
anything that happens during or after damage resolution. Moreover, if
her first theft attack causes a nymph to teleport then the second
one is aborted before noises() can be called.

